WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.438
The approval engine has three
properties that shape the design.

2
00:00:03.438 --> 00:00:05.851
Approvers don't need to hold any role.

3
00:00:05.851 --> 00:00:10.491
Approvers can't approve their own requests,
and service principals can't approve

4
00:00:10.491 --> 00:00:15.063
at all; that's enforced by the platform,
which is worth saying in the security plan.

5
00:00:15.063 --> 00:00:18.399
And approvers need a P2
or Governance license.

6
00:00:18.399 --> 00:00:20.775
From those, the separation rules.

7
00:00:20.775 --> 00:00:25.050
A tier's approvers aren't members
of that tier's eligible population;

8
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.867
otherwise the approvers and the
requesters are the same set,

9
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:31.463
and approval degrades to mutual sign-off.

10
00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:35.862
The platform blocks self-approval;
only group design blocks collusion.

11
00:00:35.862 --> 00:00:37.926
At least two approvers per tier,

12
00:00:37.926 --> 00:00:41.737
held as governed groups,
because named individuals leave.

13
00:00:41.737 --> 00:00:45.419
Tier 0 approvers sit outside
the Tier 0 population,

14
00:00:45.419 --> 00:00:47.962
for example in the security office.

15
00:00:47.962 --> 00:00:50.984
The approver's own
justification is required,

16
00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:53.687
so the record holds a
decision, not a click.

17
00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:57.513
And approver groups are reviewed like
the privileged objects they are.

18
00:00:57.513 --> 00:01:00.100
On the right, the signals and records.

19
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.825
The most important PIM alert
is roles assigned outside PIM,

20
00:01:03.825 --> 00:01:08.387
high severity, because an assignment
made outside PIM never met the gate.

21
00:01:08.387 --> 00:01:10.282
It detects rather than prevents;

22
00:01:10.282 --> 00:01:14.351
limiting who holds Privileged Role
Administrator is the prevention.

23
00:01:14.351 --> 00:01:18.526
The approval window is a fixed
twenty-four hours with a single stage,

24
00:01:18.526 --> 00:01:23.223
so a Tier 0 path staffed by two people in
one time zone will strand out-of-hours

25
00:01:23.223 --> 00:01:27.324
requests; the documented answer
to "the approvers are unavailable

26
00:01:27.324 --> 00:01:31.500
and production is down" is the
emergency procedure, not a workaround.

27
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.121
Role reviews run monthly for Tier 0
and quarterly otherwise, and never as

28
00:01:36.121 --> 00:01:41.050
self-review, because self-review leaves
role-assignable groups out entirely.

29
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:44.165
And PIM's audit history keeps thirty days,

30
00:01:44.165 --> 00:01:47.738
so the logs are exported
for AU-11 before go-live.

31
00:01:47.738 --> 00:01:52.513
One date to act on: the
legacy beta privileged-access

32
00:01:52.513 --> 00:01:56.512
API stops returning data
on October 28, 2026.

33
00:01:56.512 --> 00:01:58.870
Anything still calling it must move now.
